  "articleBody": "Deciding whether your walls need a full repaint or just some strategic touch-ups can be tricky, but knowing the key differences helps. A full interior paint package involves painting entire rooms, walls, ceilings, and often trim and doors. It’s ideal when you're looking for a completely fresh aesthetic, changing a room's vibe, or if the existing paint is significantly faded, stained, or has widespread damage. You're getting a uniform, brand-new finish. Touch-ups, on the other hand, are smaller, localized repairs to address scuffs, chips, or minor blemishes. They're great for maintaining the current look of a wall and extending the life of your paint job. The main goal is to seamlessly blend the new paint with the old. Cost and timeline are big factors, too. Touch-ups are usually quicker and less expensive, perfect for isolated issues. A full repaint is a bigger investment of time and money but delivers a transformative result. If you're dealing with widespread discoloration or damage, a full repaint is probably your best bet for a cohesive look. For minor, isolated spots, touch-ups can be a lifesaver, but remember that perfect blending isn't always guaranteed, especially with older paint.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
